  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight...Gradually increasing moisture from north to south, \nesp south of Orlando. ISOLD to SCT showers will push onto the coast\nearly this morning, esp south of the Cape, with increasing\ncoverage and intensity inland late morning/afternoon (mainly \nsouth of Melbourne). Highest PoPs 30-60% will favor southern \nOsceola and southern Brevard southward toward Lake Okeechobee and \nacross the Treasure Coast. Chances typically less than 20% north \nof Melbourne. Initial storm steering from east to west 10-15 mph, \nbut is forecast to gradually veer to out of the southeast thru the\naftn. Primary storm impacts will be lightning strikes, gusty \nwinds locally, and torrential downpours. Instability appears a bit\nlimited with 500 mb temps only -4.5 to -5.5C. By early evening, \nmost activity should be west of the Kissimmee River with mainly \ndry conditions overnight over land. Generally ERLY winds 6-12 mph \n(few higher gusts) during the day becoming light again in the \nevening. \n\nHot & humid with U80s to around 90F at the immediate coast and L90s \ninland, with some M90s near Orlando and north/west from here. Peak \nheat indices 100-107F with widespread Moderate to Major HeatRisk \nareawide. Warm & muggy overnight with persistent lows in the 70s.\n\nSat-Sun...Deep moisture (PWATs increasing > 2\") continues to take \nhold across ECFL areawide. A weak pressure gradient continues in \nplace. There should be a well-defined sea breeze collision late each \nday across the east central FL peninsula. This will promote higher \ncoverage than on Fri and further northward (30-60% - highest around \nLake Okee). Storm steering flow will be lighter this weekend with \nlikely erratic movement with stronger boundary collisions. Aside \nfrom main impacts of lightning strikes, gusty winds locally 40-55 \nmph, and torrential downpours; we may be better in line with needing \nto monitor any minor/nuisance flooding potential with a few cells. \nLight southerly winds Sat and light westerly winds Sun ahead of \nsea breeze development and slow push inland. Winds should fall to \nlight/variable each night. \n\nMax temps will be in the L-M90s each day, with U90s within play Sat \naftn near/NW of I-4. Will continue to monitor for Heat Advisory \ncriteria (108-110F for heat indices), otherwise values will continue \n104-107F ahead of precip/sea breeze passage. Widespread Moderate \nto Major HeatRisk (ISOLD Extreme) conditions will continue to \nimpact those without adequate cooling and hydration. Lows continue\nin the 70s.\n\nMon-Thu...Weak high pressure ridging influence continues thru this \nperiod, though the axis remains south of central FL. A weak\nfrontal boundary is forecast to drift south toward north FL \nnear/past mid-week. Overall moisture remains fairly high thru the\nperiod surging northward mid-week along aforementioned front. \nOverall flow offshore, with late day boundary collisions focused \nacross the eastern FL peninsula Mon and will see if the flow is \ntoo strong for the ECSB to even form Tue-Thu, though this remains \nfar enough out with lower confidence just yet. See no change in \nthe diurnal SCT-NMRS (40-70%) shower-storm pattern. Highs continue \nin the L-M90s with peak heat indices continuing to average\n102-107F each afternoon ahead of sea breeze/storms. Will continue \nto monitor as portions of ECFL could flirt with Heat Advisory \ncriteria.",
